  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 60af0432a3c869e48a591344a8dafd71, type: 3}
  m_Name: PatPre3
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  question: "In the clinic waiting room, you pick up a pamphlet about clinical trials
    for patients. Before human trials begin, you learn that scientists run several
    preclinical tests using cells grown in lab dishes or test tubes (in vitro). \r\n\r\n<b>What
    can lab-based (in vitro) experiments tell researchers about how a new treatment
    may affect patients?</b>\r\n"
  explanation: "In vitro tests are lab studies where researchers look at how a new
    treatment affects cells grown outside the body. These tests help scientists see
    how real cells respond and identify early signs of benefit or concern. While
    lab tests can\u2019t tell us everything about how a treatment will work in a
    person, they are an important first step in deciding which treatments should
    move on to animal studies and, eventually, be tested with patients in clinical
    trials."
  correctAnswer: How a treatment affects living cells, such as whether it helps them
    grow, survive, or die
  incorrectAnswer1: 'How patients feel about the possible side effects of a new treatment '
  incorrectAnswer2: How a treatment changes blood flow and heart rate in animals
  incorrectAnswer3: How a treatment is absorbed and broken down in the human body
  questionRole: Patient
  questionType: MultipleChoice
  questionStage: PreClinical
